## SKILL.md format

Write four metadata fields as top-level YAML, followed by the four
Markdown sections below. Replace placeholders with the optimization removed:

````markdown
---
skill_id: <stable-skill-id>
intent: <one brief sentence describing the optimization technique removed>
preconditions:
  - "Data types: <required types and numerical constraints; why needed>"
  - "Layout: <required contiguity, alignment, and data-to-parallel-unit mapping; why needed>"
  - "Storage: <required placement and visibility before optimization; why needed>"
  - "Pipeline: <required ordering, participation, and buffer-reuse constraints; why needed>"
  - "Hardware: <required features and capacity relationships; why needed>"
scope:
  cases: [<problem.definition.op_type>]
  languages: [<problem.language>]
  platforms: [<problem.platform>]
---

# Overview

<optimization description and replay instructions>

## Example configuration

<source-specific dimensions, stage/thread counts, and retained mechanisms;
preserve these for this replay without treating them as technique prerequisites>

# Precondition

- Data types: <expand the matching metadata entry; explain any dependency>
- Layout: <expand the matching metadata entry; explain any dependency>
- Storage: <expand the matching metadata entry; explain any dependency>
- Pipeline: <expand the matching metadata entry; explain any dependency>
- Hardware: <expand the matching metadata entry; explain any dependency>

# Scope

- Cases: <problem.definition.op_type>
- Languages: <problem.language>
- Platforms: <problem.platform>

# Code Change Snippet

## Before

```<problem.language>
<code before applying the optimization>
```

## After

```<problem.language>
<code after applying the optimization>
```
````

Intent is one brief sentence naming the optimization technique removed.
Put implementation details and Before/After code only in body.

- Overview explains what the optimization does and how to apply it.
  Include code locations, ownership, layouts, launch changes, synchronization,
  bounds handling, and numerical requirements. Use level-two subsections and
  focused code snippets when useful.
- Precondition expands the metadata prerequisites.

  - Inspect all five categories above and other relevant dependencies.
  - Data types states representation or arithmetic constraints the technique needs.
    A value's current dtype is not automatically a prerequisite for moving its bits.
  - Layout covers physical arrangement, contiguity, alignment, and ownership by
    the target's threads, warps/wavefronts, blocks, or AI cores required by the transformation.
  - Storage identifies required memory levels and visibility of input/intermediate data.
  - Pipeline states required copy/compute ordering, participation, and buffer reuse.
    Check producer completion, reader visibility, and completion before buffer reuse,
    even when the optimization preserves synchronization. Distinguish these ordering
    dependencies from incidental stage counts; do not restate the whole pipeline.
  - Hardware names features used by this technique and required capacity relationships.
    Features used only by retained computation belong in Example configuration.

- Apply these rules to both YAML preconditions and the entire # Precondition section:

  - State minimum prerequisites. For each, identify the operation or hardware rule
    in this transformation that fails without it; explain that dependency briefly.
  - A condition being true, or needing preservation in this example, is insufficient.
    If changing it only requires adapting the snippet, put it in Example configuration.
  - Keep all five categories explicit in YAML and prose: Data types, Layout, Storage,
    Pipeline, and Hardware. If a category has no additional requirement, say so and
    briefly explain why. Do not invent a dependency or substitute example settings.
  - Preserve fixed dimensions, byte strides, stage/thread counts, register budgets,
    buffer totals, and unrelated instructions in Overview / Example configuration.
    They must not reappear as requirements in # Precondition.
  - Keep intrinsic constants when justified: instruction group sizes, alignment,
    swizzle spans, and capacity formulas can be necessary dependencies.
  - Express layouts and capacity symbolically where supported. For example, a global
    row stride derives from the row extent and element size, not a fixed workload.
  - For a shared-memory permutation, trace its writer/reader layout requirements.
    WGMMA, operand dtypes, and input stage counts are not dependencies just because
    they produce the values being permuted. Identify any actual fragment constraint.
  - Expand the same dependency set in prose. Do not add instance requirements while
    expanding YAML. Keep exact replay settings and preserved mechanisms in Overview.
  - Keep storage moves, new mappings, and pipeline changes introduced by the
    optimization in Overview and After. Preconditions describe the preceding state.

- Scope renders cases, languages, and platforms as comma-separated lists.
  Derive them from the actual problem; each dimension must be nonempty.
- Code Change Snippet belongs only to body. Use Before and After code
  blocks to show the forward optimization, from deoptimized code to optimized code.
  Keep snippets focused on that change and explain any required shared context.

Construct SkillCard with these four metadata fields and body containing all four
Markdown sections. Use the registered save_skill and load_skill functions to write
SKILL.md and check its roundtrip; consult AGENTS.md for their APIs. Metadata and body
remain separate on disk; inline dictionaries include body. Keep progress and measurements
outside the skill. The recipe must work from the deoptimized kernel alone, without
upstream paths or a copy of the complete optimized kernel.
